Cursor also reads `AGENTS.md` in the project root, which carries these rules in full.
This rule file exists because `.cursor/rules` is the mechanism Cursor documents as
authoritative for project rules, and `alwaysApply: true` guarantees the essentials are
present in every request. Note that rules apply to Agent (Chat) only — not Tab
completion, not Inline Edit — so anything critical is also stated in `AGENTS.md`.

Full detail: @AGENTS.md and @CLAUDE.md, with depth in `docs/`.

## The project

Thai-language game walkthroughs, one folder per game. `games/ffx/` (Final Fantasy X) is the
reference implementation — copy what it does. Static HTML opened from disk over
`file://`: no server, no build step, no package install.

## Constraints that break the site if violated

- No `fetch` / `XHR` / ES module `import` — `file://` blocks them, and CSSOM reads on a
  `file://` stylesheet throw `SecurityError`. Use `localStorage`.
- No external images and no remote scripts. Vector art is inline `<svg>`.
- No `<style>` blocks, and no inline `style=` that sets `font-size`.
- No hardcoded hex colours inside `<svg>` — use `var(--ink)`, `var(--cyan)`,
  `currentColor`, or the colour disappears when the reader switches to dark mode.
- Content must be readable with JavaScript doing nothing.

## Language

- Prose in Thai. Technical and in-game terms stay in English and are never translated —
  `Sphere Grid`, `Overdrive`, `Save Sphere`, every item, place and character name.
- The inline separator is `" – "` (spaced en dash). `" · "` and a bare `·` are forbidden.
- Every bullet is a complete sentence that stands alone, written for a reader with zero
  prior context who will not click anything to find out what you meant.
- **A place name is not a location** — name the kind of place (`the city of Luca`), the
  route inward (building, floor, room), and how to travel there now.
- No compass directions — a player cannot tell north in-game. Use landmarks plus
  left/right relative to the direction of travel.

## Content model

A stage page reads top to bottom like a novel in strict play order: enter the scene →
where to go → what to do → what it unlocks → what you get, with the characters' mood
carried through. **Everything is inline in the scene where it happens** — a pickup is
ticked where the player walks past it, a boss block sits where the boss appears, a puzzle
walkthrough sits in the room. Only pure lookup tables (fiend list, glossary) go at the
bottom.

Three kinds of checkbox: items, milestones (`.pick milestone`), and preparation
(`.prep`). Never pad a checklist — when a scene genuinely has nothing to collect, say so
in one `data-note` line and use milestones instead.

## Accuracy

- Two independent sources for every game fact before it is written. Never guess.
- If it cannot be verified, mark it unverified or leave it out.
- Never delete or reword existing content because it looks wrong — verify first, correct
  it and say so, or leave it and report it.
- Check `docs/verified-facts/` before searching, and append whatever you newly verify.

## `data-k` is user data

Checkbox keys live in the reader's `localStorage`. Renaming or removing one erases
progress they earned by playing. Count them before and after every edit and report both
numbers. Run `node tools/sync-totals.js` after changing counts.

## Proof, not reasoning

Run `node tools/verify-all.js` before claiming anything is done. When a harness result
contradicts something you already know is fine, suspect the harness first.

## Language — split by reader, not by file type

- **Anything an agent reads is English**: `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `GEMINI.md`,
  `.github/copilot-instructions.md`, `.cursor/rules/*.mdc`, everything under `docs/`,
  every `SKILL.md`, hook source and comments, and commit messages.
- **A guide's content is written in the language that guide is for.** The Final Fantasy X
  guide is Thai; a contributor may add a guide in another language. This is the only
  place non-English prose belongs.

## Keep concepts portable — nothing hardcoded to one game

This repo holds **many** games. State the general rule and use a game as the
illustration, never the reverse. Tools discover game folders rather than naming one. A
concept that cannot be taken apart cannot be reused, so split rules until each stands
alone. Where something genuinely is game-specific — the `ffx_` `localStorage` prefix
hardcoded in the shared scripts is the live example — **say so out loud** and mark it as
work a second game must redo. The test: could a second game adopt this rule, tool or
document **without editing it**?

## Git

**Do not commit unless explicitly asked**, even when the work is finished and verified.
"The work is done" is not permission.

When you are asked, follow the `git-ship` skill in `.claude/skills/git-ship/`. Its two
load-bearing steps are the ones that get skipped otherwise: **`node tools/verify-all.js`
must exit zero before anything is committed**, and afterwards the **documentation sweep** —
re-read `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `GEMINI.md`, `README.md`, `.github/copilot-instructions.md`,
`.cursor/rules/*.mdc`, `docs/` and the skills, and confirm each still describes the repo as
it now is. Report both steps to the user **even when they find nothing**, because
"checked and clean" and "forgot to check" look identical in silence.
